    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d479">

                <pb facs="0592" n="334" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg334"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/739</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-02-17">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 17, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 5 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1662. Your 1138, February 16. Although rescinding of order forbidding publication
                    for thirty days of ships’ manifests comes at a time when the public mind and
                    press are occupied with more exciting subjects, it is appreciatively received
                    and will have excellent effect. The <hi rendition="#italic">Times</hi> this
                    morning has thankful comment.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
